{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>And if a man strikes the eye of his slave.</b> The commandment of the Torah concerning a Canaanite slave is that he goes free for a tooth and an eye, and the reason for this is that they were enslaved only by means of a tooth and an eye, as it is said (Genesis 9): ״וירא חם אבי כנען את ערות אביו ויגד לשני אחיו בחוץ״ — he saw with his eye and told with his mouth, and for this he was cursed: ״ארור כנען עבד עבדים יהיה לאחיו״; and since the sinning limbs were stricken, the body is released from the punishment of slavery. And our Rabbis of blessed memory said: ושחתה — only if he intended to destroy it; but if he intended to heal it and it was destroyed, the slave does not go free on account of it. And the ordering of the passages from וכי יכה איש את עבדו onward: it began with the killing of the slave, which is severe, and afterward is written וכי ינצו, in which is the law of the offspring, which is lighter than that, for a person does not know whether they will succeed; and afterward the law concerning the limbs of Israelites, which is ״עין תחת עין״; and afterward the law concerning the limbs of Canaanite slaves, which is the lightest of all of them; and from there onward in the laws of animals and their damages — and all is in proper order and with great providence."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:02:41.726748692Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}